A||N||2||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||CV joints?  How do you . . .?||||5||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||03-24-2001||02:36 AM||||My car has some very strange harmonics when accelerating or at certain speeds.  One of the seals at the rear shafts/transmission seems to be leaking.  And with the car on jackstands, it shakes like a cheap hotel massage bed between 45 and 70 MPH on the speedo (car is an automatic).  It is not as bad when you are driving, but there seems to be a little noise comming from the driver's side rear wheel.<BR>The bearings and suspension bushings have been replaced on both sides of the car.  <BR>When I went to remove the jackstands, the one on the driver side had grease on the side facing the driver's side inner CV joint.  <BR>It seems that I need to replace the driver's side rear drive axle.  I was thinking about nabbing one off of my parts car.<P>How hard is it to remove the axle?  <BR>What tools are needed to remove and install?||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||03-24-2001||02:52 AM||||Changing axles is a pretty easy job.  You will need an axle nut socket (a deep well 30 or 32mm....can't remember which).  <P>Once that is removed, along with the wheel, of course, you will probably need to remove the tie rod (assuming yours is a pre-'88).  You might not need to remove the tie rod, but you will need to get the splined part if the axle out of the spindle.  <P>Now take a large flat blade screwdriver or prybar (your stock lug wrench works fine) and pop the axle out of the tranny.  <P>Now is a good time to change the axle seal if it is leaky.  Just be careful......they bend/distort fairly easily.  If it's not leaking then you may want to just leave it alone.<P>Good luck.       ||||reg||1
Z||000002||V8Fiero||03-24-2001||01:31 PM||||30MM Mrfiero    <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
